were used exclusively for desalination applications, and this is, to the best of our knowledge, the first study in which TFN membranes have been used for OSN. The chosen support is crosslinked $\mathrm{P8}_{4}$ , whose synthesis was first reported by our group.4, 12 . The compatibility between polymer and filler in the resulting MMMs was enhanced due to the presence of organic ligands of MOFs. To best match the characteristic thickness of the TFN membrane, which is between 100-300 nanometers,24 the particle size of the MOFs was controlled at around 50-150 nm.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Syntheses of MOFs. The synthesis of ZIF-8 was carried out as previously reported.25 Activated ZIF-8 nanocrystals with high porosity were obtained. $N{\mathrm{H}}_{\mathrm{2}}–\mathbf{M}\mathbf{I}\mathbf{L}–53(\mathrm{Al})$ was synthesized using a modified procedure based on a previous report by Couck et al.26 MIL- ${\cdot53}(\mathrm{Al})$ was synthesized with the same composition as $N H_{2}{-}M I L{-}53(A l)$ but using terephthalic acid rather than aminoterephthalic acid as a ligand. MIL-101(Cr) was synthesized using the molar composition previously reported by Khan et al. ; however, the synthesis was carried out in a microwave oven at $\mathrm{{180~^{\circ}C}}$ for 30 min. The reduction of temperature and time in comparison with the previously reported synthesis led to a decrease in particle size from 100 nm to $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The complete details for the syntheses of MOFs can be found in the supporting information file.
Preparation of PI supports. The crosslinked PI ultrafiltration (UF) support was prepared as follows: a $24\%$ $\left(\mathbf{w}/\mathbf{w}\right)$ polymer dope solution was prepared by dissolving PI (P84, HP Polymer GmbH) in DMF (HPLC grade, VWR international) and stirring overnight. A viscous solution $(3582~\mathrm{cP}$ at ${}^{25}^{\mathrm{~\mathrm{~o~}}}\mathrm{C})$ ) was formed and allowed to stand until air bubbles disappeared. The dope solution was then cast on a polypropylene non-woven backing material taped to a glass plate using a casting knife set at a thickness of 200 $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The casting speed was $\mathbf{o.o4~ms^{-1}}$ and the casting machine was located in a room at 20 $^{\mathrm{o}}{\cal C}.$ . Immediately after casting, the membrane was immersed in a water bath (also at 20 ${}^{\mathrm{{o}}}C{\mathrm{{\Omega}}}$ ), where phase inversion occurred. After 10 min, lengths of support were transferred to a fresh water bath $({20}^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , left for $^{\textrm{\scriptsize1h}}$ and then immersed in a solvent exchange bath (isopropanol, IPA, HPLC grade, VWR international) to remove any residual water or DMF. The supports were crosslinked via immersion in a solution of hexanediamine (HDA, $99.5\%$ , Sigma Aldrich) in IPA (120 $\mathbf{g}/\mathrm{L})$ for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ at 20 $^\mathrm{{{o}}}C$ . Next, each membrane was washed with IPA four times for $^{\textrm{\scriptsize1h}}$ to remove any residual HDA. Subsequently, these supports were conditioned with polyethylene glycol (PEG, MW 400, from VWR international) to avoid pore collapse as well as the formation of PA inside the pores during the IP reaction. The conditioning was carried out by immersing the membranes in a bath comprising PEG/IPA at a volume ratio of 3:2 overnight. The membranes were then wiped dry with tissue paper.
Preparation of TFC and TFN-MOF membranes. TFC PA membranes (without filler) were hand-cast on crosslinked $\mathsf{P84}$ supports through interfacial polymerization. First, an aqueous solution of $2\%$ $\left(\mathbf{w}/\mathbf{v}\right)$ mphenylenediamine (MPD, $99\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich) and a solution of $0.1\%$ $\scriptstyle\left(\mathbf{w}/\mathbf{v}\right)$ trimesoyl chloride (TMC, $98\%$ , Sigma-Aldrich) in hexane (HPLC grade, VWR international) were prepared. The support was cut into disks of $60.8~\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and placed in a glass filtration holder for the IP reaction. For this purpose, $\mathbf{15~mL}$ of the aqueous solution was added and after 2 min the excess solution was removed and the membrane was wiped with tissue paper. Then, $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of the hexane solution was poured and after 1 min reaction time the resulting membranes were withdrawn from the hexane solution and rinsed with water.
TFN-MOF membranes were prepared following the same procedure as for TFC membranes, by dispersing typically $0.2\%\mathrm{(w/v)}$ of synthesized MOFs (ZIF-8, MIL- $53$ (Al), $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL- ${\cdot53}(\mathrm{Al})$ and MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)^{.}$ ) in the organic phase before the IP reaction. Note that for TFN-MIL-101 membranes, the MOF concentration was varied from 0.05 to $0.4\%$ $\left(\mathbf{w}/\mathbf{v}\right)$ . Nanoparticle dispersion was achieved by ultrasonication for $^{\textrm{\scriptsize1h}}$ at room temperature immediately prior to the IP reaction. Different post-treatments were carried out on TFC and TFN-ZIF-8 membranes to optimize the OSN performance, i.e. maximum flux and high rejection. Table 1 depicts 5 different post-treatments. TFN membranes with MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , NH2-MIL- ${\cdot}53^{(\mathrm{Al})}$ and MIL-53(Al) were treated via filtration with DMF $\left(\mathrm{PT}_{4}\right)$ ) as explained in the result section.
Characterization. Specific surface area measurements of ZIF-8 and MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ were performed using Micromeritics Tristar 3000 with $\Nu_{2}$ at $77.4~\mathrm{K}.$ . Prior to the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption measurement, the samples were degassed under vacuum for $^{8\mathrm{h}}$ at 200 $^\mathrm{{{o}}}C$ . The specific surface area was calculated according to the BET (Brunauer–Emmett– Teller) method. Powder XRD analyses were made using a D-Max Rigaku X-ray diffractometer with a copper anode and a graphite monochromator to select $\mathrm{CuK}\upalpha$ radiation $(\lambda{=}1.5418\mathrm{~\AA}),$ .
Thermogravimetric analyses (TGA and DTG) were performed under air flow in a TGA/SDTA 851e system (Mettler Toledo) to study the water adsorption and thermal stability of the MOFs. The analyses were conducted from $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ up to $850^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a heating rate of 10 $^{\mathrm{o}}{\mathrm{C}}/\mathrm{min}$ .
TFC/TFN membrane surfaces and MOF nanoparticles were characterized by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). SEM images were collected with a high resolution field emission gun scanning electron microscope (Carl Zeiss Ltd.) operating at $5~\mathrm{kV}$ . Samples were coated with chromium under an argon atmosphere in an Emitech $\mathsf{K}_{575}\mathrm{X}$ peltier to ensure conductivity. For EDX analysis the samples were coated with gold and analyzed in an Inspect $\mathrm{~F~}$ scanning electron microscope. To verify the presence of MOFs in the TFN, membrane cross sectional and surface images were taken by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), with a Tecnai T20 operating at 200 kV. To obtain membrane cross sectional images, a lamella of the top $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ of the membrane was prepared using the Focused Ion Beam technique (FIB, Dual Beam 3 Nova 200), where the membrane, previously coated with platinum, was cut by accelerating concentrated gallium ions (30 KV, 50 pA) to a specific site. Once the lamella was created, it was lifted-out by a micromanipulator and glued on a support (carbon copper grid) where the final thinning (150 nm thickness) took place. For the membrane surface images, TFN-MIL-101 membranes were synthesized on an alumina support (20 nm pore size) instead of on crosslinked $\mathrm{P8}_{4}$ support. The PA-MOF skin layer was detached from the support by floating on water and the film was placed on a carbon copper grid.
TFN-MIL-101 membrane surface composition (the top 60 nm) was analyzed using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). XPS was performed in an AXIS ultra DLD (Kratos Tech.) analysis system, using a monochromated AlKα $(\mathrm{1486.6~eV})$ excitation at 12 $\mathbf{kV}$ and 10 mA. Prior to measurement, cleaned surface was obtained by spraying the sample with an ion beam of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{r}^{+}$ operating at $3\ensuremath{\mathrm{\keV}}$ and with an emission current of $5\mathrm{mA}$ . To profile the concentration of MOF in the first nanometer layers, an etching ion gun was used (beam energy of $_{3}\mathrm{\keV,\5\mathrm{\mA}}$ ), taking data every hour. Data processing was performed using Casa XPS.
The wettability of the membranes was determined by contact angle analysis using a Drop Shape Analyzer (DSA 10 MK2, Krüss, Germany) at 20 $^{\circ}C$ . The volume of the water droplets was 20 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ and at least 5 measurements were performed on each membrane sample. The samples were immersed in IPA and dried in air before the measurements.
ATR-FTIR was performed in a Bruker Vertex 70 spectrophotometer with a DTGS detector and Golden Gate accessory. The measurements were run from 600 to 4000 $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ with a resolution of $2~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ .
Membrane performance. Nanofiltration experiments were carried out in a pump pressurized cross-flow unit provided with 8 cells connected in series. The performance of the membranes was evaluated using two different solvents, tetrahydrofuran (THF, HPLC grade, VWR International), and methanol (MeOH, HPLC grade, VWR International), with kinetic diameters of $_{0.63}$ and 0.36 nm, respectively. Standard feed solutions comprising a homologous series of styrene oligomers (PS, $\bf{1}\mathrm{~\gL^{-1}}$ of $\mathrm{PS}_{5}80$ and PS990, from Polymer Labs, and 0.01 $\mathrm{gL}^{-1}$ of $\upalpha$ - methylstyrene dimer, from Sigma-Aldrich) dissolved in the selected solvents were used for nanofiltration. Styrene oligomers were analyzed in the permeate and feed samples using an Agilent HPLC system with UV-Vis detector set at a wavelength of $264~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . All the experiments were carried out at 30 bar and a feed flow of 100 $\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ . The effective membrane area was $\mathbf{14~cm}^{2}$ . Permeate samples for flux
